## What we are doing

Make `sglang_decode_internal_bench_and_profiling` able to run a batch whose requests have
**different input lengths**. Today `--input-len` is one scalar and batch-uniformity is asserted
every iteration, so a ragged batch is not merely unsupported — it is actively rejected.

**Plan 1, chosen by the user:** every attention-DP rank receives the **same ISL multiset in the
same order**. All ranks stay in lockstep, so the cross-rank determinism check survives intact
rather than being deleted. (Plan 2 — split a global distribution unevenly across ranks — was
considered and rejected: it would cost the harness its strongest invariant.)

### Four ways to specify the ISL multiset

| mode | spec | note |
|---|---|---|
| uniform | `70000` or `uniform:70000` | today's behaviour, must stay byte-compatible |
| bimodal | `bimodal:8192,70000,0.25` | **minority side rounds UP (ceil)** |
| normal | `normal:40000,8000[,lo,hi]` | clamp by saturation; realized list recorded |
| list | `list:a,b,c,...` or `list:@file` | most general; the other three reduce to it |

The spec describes the **per-rank** batch (`local_batch_size = batch_size // dp_size`), not the
global one.

## The four findings that shrink this task

Established first-hand on 2026-09-15 before work began. Do not re-derive.

**F1. The attention backend needs zero changes.** `dsa_backend.py:1143-1153` takes `seq_lens` as a
per-request tensor and derives `max_seqlen_k` via `.max()`. Ragged batches are the normal serving
path. CUDA graphs key on batch size, not sequence length.

**F2. `len(set(accept_lens)) == 1` is TRUE and must be KEPT.** `spec_utils.py:411-419` draws one
scalar `simulate_acc_len` per iteration and broadcasts it across the batch. That uniformity is
upstream behaviour and is what makes `realized_accept_length` bit-reproducible. Only the
`len(set(previous_lens)) == 1` half of `topology.py:69` is what has to go.

**F3. KV allocation is already decoupled from the logical ISL.** `batch.prepare_for_extend()` runs
*before* the per-request truncation loop, over a uniform max row width. Short requests simply
over-reserve. **Consequence to state in the report: a mixed batch costs the same KV as an all-max
batch.** Changing that means changing the allocation path and is out of scope.

**F4. `validate_worker_progress` is already elementwise** — zero changes.

## Core principles for this task

**Determinism is the product.** `realized_accept_length = 3.6134393063583814` (EP1, C=256,
ISL 70000) is the regression gate. A uniform-mode run that does not reproduce it exactly is a bug,
however good its TPOT looks. Baseline TPOT for reference: **25.6740 ms**, `verify_iterations 2768`.

**The ISL generator must not touch any global RNG.** It owns a private `random.Random(seed)`.
Perturbing `random` / `numpy.random` / `torch` would shift the acceptance coin sequence and break
the gate above.

**Suspend, don't conclude.** Whether a heterogeneous batch's TPOT is interpretable at all is an
**open question** — DSA is sparse with `index_topk = 2048` and the per-step cost-vs-length
relationship has not been measured. Do not predict it and do not design around a guess about it.

**A backend that was requested is not a backend that ran.** Any GPU run asserts from the log which
kernel actually executed. `dsa_backend.py:257` records that this exact silent fallback once cost
27 hours.

